import{_ as o,o as t,c as r,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"The TypeScript 7 native server is found per root, ahead of every other pick","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0044-the-typescript-7-native-server-is-found-per-root.md","filePath":"adr/0044-the-typescript-7-native-server-is-found-per-root.md","lastUpdated":1792140001000}'),a={name:"adr/0044-the-typescript-7-native-server-is-found-per-root.md"};function c(n,e,i,d,p,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="the-typescript-7-native-server-is-found-per-root-ahead-of-every-other-pick" tabindex="-1">The TypeScript 7 native server is found per root, ahead of every other pick <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-typescript-7-native-server-is-found-per-root-ahead-of-every-other-pick" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The TypeScript 7 native server is found per root, ahead of every other pick&quot;">​</a></h1><p>TypeScript 7 is the native Go rewrite of the compiler. It dropped <code>tsserver</code>, and <code>typescript-language-server</code> therefore cannot serve a TypeScript 7 project. TypeScript 7 carries its own Language server inside the compiler binary: <code>tsc --lsp --stdio</code>. A TypeScript 7 project is a project whose <code>node_modules</code> installs <code>typescript@7</code>, usually with no TypeScript server on <code>PATH</code> at all. Before this ADR the registry reported such a project absent, and an operator override of <code>node_modules/.bin/tsc</code> only worked when pi happened to start in exactly that project root.</p><p><strong>Detection walks up from the session root for the project&#39;s own <code>typescript</code> package and reads its version.</strong> Major 7 or newer is the native compiler: its <code>node_modules/.bin/tsc</code> serves the root with the arguments <code>--lsp --stdio</code>, ahead of every machine-level binary, because it is the project&#39;s real compiler and no other server can drive it. A nearer install below major 7 stops the walk: for that tree the classic <code>typescript-language-server</code> remains the right server even when an outer directory holds a newer one. A file whose own root carries a <code>typescript@7</code> the session-root walk cannot see is still served by that root&#39;s local <code>tsc</code>. A <code>tsc</code> on <code>PATH</code> is accepted only after a version probe, cached per command, reports 7 or newer: the 5.x and 6.x <code>tsc</code> is a compiler CLI that rejects <code>--lsp</code>. <code>tsgo</code> on <code>PATH</code> is the native preview and counts without a probe.</p><p><strong>Server commands resolve to absolute paths before the spawn.</strong> A settings <code>command</code> is first looked up on <code>PATH</code>, then resolved against the session root. The server process then runs from its root with an absolute command, so a hoisted <code>node_modules</code> and a session started anywhere both work.</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> Pinning a <code>typescript-language-server</code> version that understands TypeScript 7. Rejected: no such server exists, because the node-based language service TypeScript 7 removed is what that server drives. Detecting TypeScript 7 only through a settings override. Rejected: it leaves the default experience broken on the most common shape of a TypeScript 7 machine, where nothing is on <code>PATH</code> and the project carries its own compiler. Probing every candidate with <code>tsc --lsp --stdio</code> (handshake and kill) instead of reading the package version. Rejected: a real spawn per registry resolution, and the registry resolves on nearly every hook.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> One machine can serve a TypeScript 5 project with <code>typescript-language-server</code> and a TypeScript 7 project with the native <code>tsc</code> in the same session: the pick is per root, and a project that pins an older TypeScript keeps the classic server. A <code>typescript@7</code> install without its <code>tsc</code> bin is a broken install and is reported absent, not worked around. The version probe runs at most once per command per session. The <code>--lsp</code> flag is hidden from <code>tsc --help</code> upstream; the two-flag form <code>--lsp --stdio</code> is the only form that runs, and the extension always sends both. An operator <code>command</code> override always wins over the auto-detection, and an override that names <code>tsc</code> or <code>tsgo</code> without <code>args</code> receives the native arguments.</p>',6)])])}const u=o(a,[["render",c]]);export{v as __pageData,u as default};
